<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Publication Ethics & Editorial Policy - AJSMR</title><style>body{font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;margin:0;background:#f5f6f8;color:#222;line-height:1.65}.container{max-width:1050px;margin:35px auto;padding:0 20px}.card{background:#fff;border-radius:8px;padding:32px;box-shadow:0 2px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.08)}h1{margin-top:0;font-size:30px}h2{font-size:21px;margin-top:28px}h3{font-size:18px;margin-top:22px}.note{background:#f0f4f8;padding:14px 16px;border-left:4px solid #555;margin:20px 0}.small{font-size:13px;color:#666}</style></head><body><div class="container"><div class="card"><h1>Publication Ethics & Editorial Policy</h1>
<div class="note">AJSMR is committed to responsible scholarly publishing, transparent editorial decision-making, research integrity and protection of the published record. This policy is intended to describe the practices actually followed by the journal.</div>
<h2>1. Editorial Responsibility and Independence</h2>
<p>The Editor-in-Chief has overall responsibility for the journal's editorial direction and publication decisions. Decisions should be based on scholarly merit, relevance to the journal's scope, methodological quality, originality and ethical compliance, and should not be influenced improperly by commercial or personal interests.</p>
<h2>2. Fair and Non-Discriminatory Assessment</h2>
<p>Manuscripts are assessed without discrimination based on personal characteristics, nationality, institutional affiliation or other irrelevant considerations. Legitimate editorial requirements and applicable law remain applicable.</p>
<h2>3. Peer Review</h2>
<p>Primary research articles are subject to external peer review before publication. AJSMR's published peer-review policy describes the review model, reviewer selection, confidentiality, conflicts of interest and decision process. The editorial process stated on the website should correspond to the process actually used.</p>
<h2>4. Confidentiality</h2>
<p>Editors, reviewers and editorial staff must treat submitted manuscripts and associated information as confidential. Unpublished material must not be used for personal advantage or disclosed to unauthorized persons.</p>
<h2>5. Conflicts of Interest</h2>
<p>Authors, reviewers and editors should disclose relevant conflicts of interest. An editor should not handle a manuscript where a conflict could compromise impartiality. Appropriate reassignment should be used where necessary.</p>
<h2>6. Authorship and Contributor Responsibility</h2>
<p>Authorship should reflect substantial contributions to the work. All listed authors should approve the submitted version and accept responsibility for their contributions. Changes to authorship after submission should be documented and agreed by the affected authors.</p>
<h2>7. Research Ethics</h2>
<p>Research involving human participants, human data or animals should comply with applicable ethical requirements. Manuscripts should include relevant ethics approval, consent and registration information where required.</p>
<h2>8. Research Integrity</h2>
<p>AJSMR does not tolerate plagiarism, fabrication, falsification, inappropriate image manipulation, duplicate publication or other practices that compromise research integrity. Concerns are handled under the journal's research-integrity procedures.</p>
<h2>9. Corrections, Expressions of Concern and Retractions</h2>
<p>When a published article contains a material error or integrity problem, the journal may publish a correction, expression of concern or retraction as appropriate. Notices should clearly identify the affected article and explain the reason, while maintaining a transparent scholarly record.</p>
<h2>10. Complaints and Appeals</h2>
<p>Authors and other stakeholders may raise complaints or appeal editorial decisions using the journal's published complaints and appeals procedure. Concerns involving a conflicted editor should be handled by an appropriate independent editorial authority or publisher representative.</p>
<h2>11. Reviewer and Editor Misconduct</h2>
<p>Concerns about reviewer or editor conduct may be investigated by the journal or publisher. Where appropriate, the journal may request evidence, seek institutional input or take corrective action.</p>
<h2>12. Artificial Intelligence</h2>
<p>AI tools must not be listed as authors. Authors remain responsible for the accuracy, originality and integrity of their submissions. Reviewers and editors must protect confidential manuscript information and should not use external generative AI systems in ways that compromise confidentiality. See the journal's dedicated AI & Generative AI Policy.</p>
<h2>13. Ethical Frameworks</h2>
<p>AJSMR may draw on recognized publication-ethics principles and guidance, including the Committee on Publication Ethics (COPE), the World Association of Medical Editors (WAME), and relevant biomedical publishing recommendations, as applicable to the journal.</p>
<h2>14. Editorial Records</h2>
<p>The journal should maintain appropriate editorial records to support accountability, peer-review documentation, publication decisions, corrections and the integrity of the scholarly record.</p>
<h2>15. Policy Transparency</h2>
<p>This policy is publicly available and should be reviewed when the journal's editorial practices change. The website should not make claims about procedures that the journal does not actually follow.</p><p class="small">AJSMR local testing version. Final publication should be checked against actual journal procedures before deployment to the live website.</p></div></div></body></html>